WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
ai just tried solving the Fermi paradox and built a fantasy transport game you can actually play. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 224. Deep dive walks the checking loop, dread and district puts logistics against evil, and a chat plan greens a lawn. Researcher Ethan Mollick, sharing the full write-up on the where is everybody Fermi page, gave two different ai models the same giant challenge. An ai model is software that predicts what should come next, like autocomplete on your phone, except it has

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.860
gotten good enough to gather facts and argue through a problem.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.080
The challenge was the Fermi paradox.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.240
The puzzle of why we have found no signs of alien life even though the universe is enormous

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:40.800
and old.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.900
He asked Fable 5.2 and Gemini Carbon to gather data.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.760
Test possible explanations.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.080
Pressure tests those ideas against tough critics and give likely answers if they could not reach

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.120
a definite conclusion.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.960
Pressure tests just means trying to break your own idea on purpose.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.460
the way you double check a math answer by working it backwards.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.540
The models did not solve the paradox completely.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.980
They gathered real data from astronomy and biology, then built several possible explanations,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.440
such as civilizations being too far apart, or civilizations not lasting long enough.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.500
Picture a huge party where you sent invitations weeks ago, the whole neighborhood should have

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.440
shown up, and the door never opens.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.360
That empty room feeling is the Fermi paradox in everyday clothes.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.140
The AI has treated each explanation the way a detective treats a suspect list.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.560
They asked what evidence would knock a suspect out, then looked for that evidence.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:41.000
A student curious about space now has a thinking partner that can pull facts together and walk through possibilities.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.180
The way a patient tutor lays index cards on a table instead of dumping a textbook on you.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.300
Even strong models still work best when a human guides the questions and judges the results.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.440
One starting prompt turned into a structured report that anyone can read and compare against

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.120
their own ideas about the universe.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.640
Go to the where is everybody Fermi site, scroll the hypotheses they tested, and notice

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:04.960
which ones feel convincing.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.940
Watch which ones fall apart when you try to break them.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.560
The page shows the full chain of reasoning so you can follow along and decide for yourself

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:13.300
what makes sense.

00:02:14.399 --> 00:02:18.639
You know that slow dread when your phone dies by lunch, and you start listing reasons?

00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:24.699
Maybe the screen stayed on. Maybe one game drank the battery. Maybe the charger is quietly failing.

00:02:25.219 --> 00:02:29.539
You do not stop at the first guess. You check each reason against what actually happened.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:35.239
That same habit stretched across a much bigger mystery is how these models work the alien question.

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:38.239
They did not blurt one clever sentence and freeze.

00:02:38.859 --> 00:02:43.819
First they built several possible explanations. Next they asked what would disprove each idea,

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:46.259
then hunted for evidence that could break the guess.

00:02:46.259 --> 00:02:50.739
Yes, if one explanation survived the checks better than the others, it got ranked higher.

00:02:51.259 --> 00:02:55.639
The weaker ones stayed on the page anyway, so you could compare them yourself, like keeping

00:02:55.639 --> 00:02:58.579
every draft of an essay instead of shredding the messy ones.

00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:00.139
The key move is the loop.

00:03:00.659 --> 00:03:05.659
The model says, here is my claim, here is why it might be wrong, here is new data that tests

00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:07.339
it, then it goes around again.

00:03:07.899 --> 00:03:12.199
That loop is what turns simple autocomplete into something that can explore an open mystery,

00:03:12.199 --> 00:03:16.079
entry, including ideas like hidden signals set against real observations.

00:03:16.519 --> 00:03:21.059
Because every pass stays visible, a beginner can follow the chain and see where the reasoning

00:03:21.059 --> 00:03:22.079
holds up or cracks.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:26.919
The result feels less like a magic trick and more like a careful notebook someone else

00:03:26.919 --> 00:03:28.019
filled in for you to review.

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:30.439
You still pick which answers feel right.

00:03:30.999 --> 00:03:34.659
Once you can see the loop, the giant question stops looking like a locked door.

00:03:35.676 --> 00:03:40.856
Claude Fable 5.2 took one short paragraph describing a transport simulation game with

00:03:40.856 --> 00:03:46.156
heart, and turned it into a complete playable experience. Several AI agents then argued

00:03:46.156 --> 00:03:50.436
about the art style, the story lore, and the jokes until everything fit together into

00:03:50.436 --> 00:03:52.336
a working game called Dread and District.

00:03:52.936 --> 00:03:58.076
Agents here just mean separate AI helpers splitting the jobs, like a group project where one

00:03:58.076 --> 00:04:02.916
person fights for the look, one guards the story, and one keeps punching up the jokes until

00:04:02.916 --> 00:04:08.016
the whole thing clicks. The result feels like a city builder mixed with an adventure, except

00:04:08.016 --> 00:04:13.256
moving goods efficiently is literally how you beat the bad guys. Think of defeating the final boss

00:04:13.256 --> 00:04:18.656
by running the best delivery service in town. The way a well-planned Minecraft supply line quietly

00:04:18.656 --> 00:04:24.016
wins the world. No coding was needed from the person who started it. The creator gave the model

00:04:24.016 --> 00:04:29.456
of vague paragraph and let the agents handle world building and balance, like handing friends a campfire

00:04:29.456 --> 00:04:35.136
spark and watching them build the whole campsite. For anyone who likes games or storytelling, a single

00:04:35.136 --> 00:04:39.736
creative spark can become something you can actually play in minutes, an experiment that

00:04:39.736 --> 00:04:44.776
used to need whole teams of artists and programmers. The game stays light and focused on logistics

00:04:44.776 --> 00:04:50.296
versus evil, so it stays clear even on a phone screen. Open the dread and district site

00:04:50.296 --> 00:04:55.156
on your phone or laptop and play a few turns. Watch how the game teaches its rules through

00:04:55.156 --> 00:05:00.116
the world itself instead of a long tutorial. The way you learn Minecraft by punching a tree,

00:05:00.416 --> 00:05:04.516
not by reading a manual first. See if you can find the moment when efficient

00:05:04.516 --> 00:05:07.176
Transport choices start to change the story outcome.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.240
One person on Reddit took a photo of their brown lawn and asked chat GPT.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.720
The chat bot many people already use for homework and ideas, for a step-by-step fixed plan.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.140
They followed the exact instructions about timing, watering, and products, and the grass is

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:27.920
now green and healthy again, without sitting through hours of YouTube videos pushing specific

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:28.320
brands.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:33.900
They described the problem, got a tailored schedule, and saw results after applying the steps

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.840
at the right times of day and year. More like a neighbor scribbling a plan on a sticky note than

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:44.240
a commercial shouting buy this bag. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.680
huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. If this

